
<p>Chan Karunamuni, human interface designer at Apple, on X:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>iPhone Duo is here! Excited to have worked on the design of our
open/close transition. It’s designed to make the two displays feel
like one seamless experience by perceptually bending the focal
plane, not the content, so that it feels like opening a window
onto a singular view.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Karunamuni thanks a few engineers by name, which is always good (and for Apple employees, far too rare) to see.</p>

<p><a href="https://daringfireball.net/search/johnnie+manzari">Johnnie Manzari</a>, HI designer formerly of Apple, now at Google DeepMind, <a href="https://x.com/johnnie/status/2113643318165082486">retweeting Karunamuni</a>:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>The second I saw that transition I thought “here’s the next
‘change the whole industry’ design from Chan”.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Previous such raise-the-bar designs that Karunamuni led or played a major hand in include <a href="https://x.com/chan_k/status/1570795751764295682">the Dynamic Island</a> in <a href="https://x.com/chan_k/status/1571207749677219840">2022</a>, <a href="https://x.com/chan_k/status/1703820400902431077">the “shockwave moment”</a> when you put two iPhones together to initiate AirDrop or NameDrop in 2023, and iOS 18’s <a href="https://x.com/chan_k/status/1835729051178451013">cool-ass Flashlight beam UI</a> in 2024. He also gave Steve Lemay <a href="https://x.com/chan_k/status/1996825927561658854">this full-throated public endorsement</a> upon his promotion to lead all of Apple HI design upon Alan Dye’s departure for Meta last December.</p>

